import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Why I started LightPhotos — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="A Lightroom license that stopped working, years of photos I couldn't open, and the afternoon I gave up and built the small editor I actually needed."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-06-23">June 23, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Why I started LightPhotos</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          A Lightroom license that stopped working, years of photos I couldn't
          open, and the afternoon I gave up and built the small editor I
          actually needed.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 5 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          I paid for Lightroom, then an OS upgrade quietly killed it. I lost
          years of photos to a library format only one app could read. The free
          editors wanted me to learn a whole craft when I just wanted to send
          some pictures to my friends.
        </p>
        <p>
          So one afternoon I wrote down seven things and built the smallest
          editor that would do them. It has also turned into how I'm embracing
          coding agents to build software, and doing it with care rather than
          speed.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <p>
        If you only read this far, these are the four things I'd want you to
        take away.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Keep your photos as plain files.</strong> A backup only helps
          you if something can still open it. Ordinary files on a drive can be
          opened by any app, today or in ten years. I learned this the
          expensive way.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Buying software once doesn't mean you own it.</strong> An OS
          upgrade can end a license just as surely as cancelling a
          subscription, and nobody will warn you.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>More features can make a tool worse.</strong> The free
          editors could do everything, and that is exactly what made them
          impossible for me to pick up.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write the list first.</strong> Deciding on seven things up
          front is what let me say no to everything else, and it's the single
          habit that has mattered most since.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Nothing quite fit</h2>
      <p>
        I took some pictures with my Sony full-frame camera. I shoot RAW, so
        most of them needed a little work before I could share them. That
        should be a five-minute problem. It took me a week, and none of it was
        editing.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 224" role="img" aria-label="Five options and why each one failed. Lightroom Classic: the installer stopped running after a macOS upgrade. Luminar AI: not the keyboard-first way I edit. Apple Photos: beautiful and fast, with one deal breaker I can't get past. Darktable and friends: everything I needed behind everything I didn't. A Lightroom subscription: ten dollars a month for something I open a few times a year. All five lead to building a small one myself.">
