        <figcaption>
          A week of evaluating software to avoid an afternoon of writing it.
          I've made worse trades.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>The one I actually loved</h2>
      <p>
        I want to slow down on Apple Photos, because it doesn't belong with the
        others on that list. I really like Apple Photos. It's beautiful, it's
        fast, and it does very nearly everything I wanted. If you use it and
        you're happy, I completely understand why.
      </p>
      <p>
        There's one deal breaker, and it's a big one. Your whole library lives
        in a single file, in a format only Apple Photos can read. I found out
        what that means the hard way. A newer version of macOS refused to open
        Photos libraries from my older backups, and several years of pictures
        went with it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The backups were fine. They were sitting right there on the drive. The
        only app that could read them just wouldn't.
      </p>
      <p>
        That's the part I can't get past, and it has nothing to do with the
        features or the speed. Everything good about it sits on top of a format
        I don't control, and I'm not willing to make that trade a second time.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 208" role="img" aria-label="Two paths for the same photos. Locked in a library: your photos become one library file and are gone after an upgrade. Kept as ordinary files: your photos stay ordinary files and you can open them in anything.">
