          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="400" y1="73" x2="422" y2="73"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M422 68 L432 73 L422 78 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x="432" y="58" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="520" y="78" text-anchor="middle">Gone after an upgrade</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="116" width="640" height="84" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="138">Kept as ordinary files</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="150" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="104" y="170" text-anchor="middle">Your photos</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="192" y1="165" x2="214" y2="165"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M214 160 L224 165 L214 170 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="224" y="150" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="312" y="170" text-anchor="middle">Ordinary files</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="400" y1="165" x2="422" y2="165"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M422 160 L432 165 L422 170 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="432" y="150" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="520" y="170" text-anchor="middle">Open them in anything</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Same photos, same backup drive. The only difference is whether one
          company's file format sits between you and them.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        It doesn't fit how I keep things either. My main drive is for recent
        work, and older shoots move off to other drives. I don't want hundreds
        of gigabytes sitting on my laptop, locked inside a library I might not
        be able to open in a few years.
      </p>

      <h2>So I built one</h2>
      <p>
        That afternoon I sat down to write a basic version myself. I wasn't
        trying to clone Lightroom. I wanted the smallest thing that would get
        these photos to my friends, and I wrote the list down before I wrote
        any code.
      </p>
      <ol class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Open any folder and show the photos in it.</strong> No import
          step and no library file. My photos stay as ordinary files on
          whatever drive holds them, so I can always open them with something
          else.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rate photos from 1 to 5,</strong> so I can go through a shoot
          quickly and mark the keepers.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Filter by rating,</strong> so I only see the photos I like.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Adjust exposure, highlights, shadows and the other basics.</strong>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep every adjustment non-destructive.</strong> The original
          file is never touched, so I can always undo an edit or start over.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Export selected photos to JPEG,</strong> ready to send.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Run on a Mac.</strong>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        That's all it had to do. Anything not on that list didn't make it into
        the first version, and writing it down is the only reason I was able to
        keep saying no.
      </p>

      <h2>What I'm really after</h2>
      <p>
        Here's the part I didn't expect when I started, and it's the reason
        I've kept going long after those photos were sent.
      </p>
      <p>
        I don't write much of the code. Coding agents do. That's easy to say
        and much harder to do well, because an agent will happily hand you
        something that compiles, runs, and is quietly wrong or quietly slow. A
        photo editor is an unusually honest place to find that out. If a slider
        lags or a folder of RAW files eats your memory, you feel it
        immediately. There's nowhere to hide.
      </p>
      <p>
        So LightPhotos is two projects for me at once. It's the photo editor I
        wanted, and it's how I'm working out what it takes to build software
        with agents and still care about quality and speed. That's what these
        posts are mostly about.
      </p>

      <h2>What comes next</h2>
      <p>
        In the next post I'll walk through the architectural choices behind
        LightPhotos and what I gave up for each one. If any of this sounds
        familiar, I'd genuinely like to hear how you solved it.
      </p>

